course: adult-life
lesson: 4
title: When work goes wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Sometimes work goes wrong: a firm cuts jobs, a factory closes, someone is sacked, workers go on strike, or a grown-up is simply worn out.
explain: Grown-ups often use soft words for these things, like "letting people go" or "between jobs", so it is worth knowing what they really mean.
explain: Picture a kitchen in Adelaide: Dad comes off the phone pale and says "They're letting people go", and later that night the kids hear Mum and Dad talking quietly about saving money in case it's him.
explain: These things are worrying, but they are almost never anyone's fault at home, and most families get through them.
explain: There are also ways workers stand up for themselves: unions, strikes, picket lines and tribunals.
fact: "Letting people go" means a company is ending some workers' jobs, usually to cut costs; it sounds gentle but people lose their jobs.
fact: Redundancy means the job itself is being got rid of, not that the person did anything wrong; people who have worked there a while often get some money to help them.
fact: Being sacked or fired means losing a job because of something the person did, such as often being late or breaking rules; employers usually have to give warnings first.
fact: "Between jobs" is a softer way of saying unemployed, wanting work but not having any.
fact: While a parent is unemployed the family may claim benefits and cut spending; free fun like parks and libraries helps.
fact: A trade union is a group of workers who each pay a small fee so they can talk to bosses together about pay and conditions.
fact: One worker alone has little power, but many together are much harder to ignore.
fact: A strike is when workers agree together to stop working to push their employer about pay or conditions; they are not paid while on strike.
fact: In the UK a strike is legal after a vote, and the disruption is deliberate so that people notice.
fact: A picket line is striking workers standing outside their workplace with signs, peacefully asking others not to go in.
fact: Burnout means being worn down by too much work and stress for too long, until even small tasks feel huge.
fact: When a business closes, everyone who works there loses their job; if it was a big employer, a whole town feels it.
fact: An employment tribunal is a kind of court for work disputes, such as being sacked unfairly or not being paid what is owed.
exercise: Dad on the phone: "They're letting people go." What does it mean? | The company is ending some workers' jobs | The company is giving everyone a long day off with pay | The company is letting workers go home early | The company is letting people choose their hours
exercise: Why do firms let people go? | Usually to cut costs when sales fall | Because the workers asked to leave all at once | To punish the workers who were often late | Because the law says jobs end after a year
exercise: You overhear that Dad's firm is letting people go. What is a good thing to do? | Ask a grown-up if it affects your family | Keep it secret and worry about it alone at night | Tell everyone at school that Dad has been sacked | Pretend you didn't hear a single word of it
exercise: "She's been made redundant." What does it mean? | Her job has been got rid of, not because of anything she did | She was sacked for breaking the rules again and again at work | She has been promoted to a new job | She has retired early with a party
exercise: "He got the sack." What does it mean? | He lost his job because of something he did | He was given a big bag of presents on his birthday | His job was cut to save money | He left to start his own business
exercise: What is the difference between redundancy and the sack? | Redundancy is about the job going, the sack is about what the person did | They are exactly the same thing | Redundancy is worse because it is always your own fault, and the sack is not | The sack means you get extra money
exercise: Someone at school says a neighbour "got the sack". What should you do? | Not spread it around | Tell the whole class at school | Ask the neighbour what he did wrong | Make a joke about it at lunch
exercise: "Your dad's between jobs at the moment." What does it mean? | Dad is out of work and looking for a new job | Dad is doing two jobs at once and is very busy | Dad is on holiday between two trips | Dad works in the space between two offices
exercise: Dad is between jobs. Which is true? | He is the same person, and free fun like parks helps | He must have been bad at his last job and everyone knows it | The family will never have a job again | He should be ashamed and hide it
exercise: "I'm joining the union." What is a trade union? | A group of workers who talk to bosses together about pay and conditions | A club for people who collect trains | A team of bosses who run a company and decide everybody's pay together | A government office that finds people jobs
exercise: Why do workers join a union? | Many workers together are harder to ignore than one | Unions pay all their members' wages every month | It is the only way to get a job | Unions let members skip work whenever they like
exercise: The TV news: "Train drivers will strike on Monday." What does it mean? | The drivers will stop work to push for better pay or conditions | The drivers will hit the trains | The trains will run faster on Monday | The drivers will be sacked on Monday and new drivers will take over
exercise: Are workers paid while they are on strike? | No, they are not paid while on strike | Yes, they get double pay | Yes, the government pays them instead of the boss | Only if the strike is on a Monday
exercise: "We drove past the picket line at the hospital." What is a picket line? | Striking workers standing outside their workplace with signs | A fence around the hospital car park | A queue of people waiting to see a doctor at the front door | A line painted on the road for ambulances
exercise: "I can't keep doing this, I'm burnt out." What does burnout mean? | Worn down by too much work and stress for too long | Burnt their hand on the oven at work and had to go home | Ran out of petrol on the way to work | Very excited about a new project
exercise: "The factory's closing down." Why might a whole town feel it? | Everyone who worked there loses their job at once | Factories pay for all the town's schools | The town will have to move somewhere else | Nobody will be able to buy anything in the town ever again
exercise: "I'm taking them to a tribunal." What is an employment tribunal? | A kind of court for work disputes | A meeting to choose a new boss | A union party for workers | A special kind of job interview
test: Overheard: "Fifty jobs going at the depot, Dave's worried he's next." Why is Dave worried? | When a firm lets people go, those who stay fear they will be next | Dave is the boss and must sack fifty people | Dave wants to leave but can't | Dave is going to get a promotion
test: Your sister says: "Mum got made redundant, so she must have messed up." Put her right. | Redundancy means the job was cut, not that she did anything wrong | She is right, redundancy is for bad workers | It means Mum quit because she was bored | It means Mum will get exactly the same job back again tomorrow morning
test: Overheard: "Late every single day for a month, no wonder they fired him." What happened? | He lost his job because of what he did | His job was cut to save money and he did nothing wrong | He went on strike with his workmates for a month | He was made redundant and given a payment
test: Nan, whispering: "He's between jobs, so don't go asking him about work at the party." What does she mean? | He is out of work and might feel sensitive about it | He is moving from one office to another across town | He has two jobs and is very busy | He is a famous person who won't talk
test: Your friend says: "Strikes are illegal, they're just being naughty." Put her right. | In the UK a strike is legal after a vote | She is right, strikes always break the law | Strikes are only legal in the week before Christmas | Only bosses and managers are allowed to strike
test: Uncle at the barbecue: "Bloody rail strike, I'll be stuck at home Monday." Why is the strike annoying for him? | The disruption is deliberate so people notice | The strike was organised against him personally by his neighbours | He will have to drive the trains himself | Strikes make the trains free
test: Dad: "On my own the boss wouldn't listen. With the union behind me, he had to." Why? | Many workers together are harder to ignore than one | The union paid the boss to listen | The union sacked the boss | The boss is a member of the union too
test: Mum, slumped on the sofa: "Can't even decide what to cook. Done in. Months of this." What might be wrong? | Burnout from too much stress for too long | She doesn't like cooking any more | She is cross with the children | She is planning a big surprise party for the family
test: Mum is burnt out. Which is true? | It is not your fault, but small kindnesses and rest help | You must fix it by doing all her work for her every single day | It will pass if everyone ignores it | It means she is about to be sacked
test: News: "Steelworks to close, 2,000 jobs lost; town in shock." Why is the whole town in shock? | So many people lose their jobs at once | The steelworks was the only house in town | Steel will no longer exist anywhere in the world | The town must close too
test: Overheard: "They never paid me for those three months. I'm going to a tribunal." What does she hope for? | A fair ruling that she gets the pay she is owed | To be given a promotion | To have her boss sent to prison for many years | To get her old job back as boss
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the distinction between redundancy and dismissal? | Redundancy concerns the abolition of the post; dismissal concerns the conduct of the employee | Redundancy and dismissal are synonymous terms for voluntary resignation | Dismissal concerns the abolition of the post; redundancy concerns the conduct of the employee concerned | Both terms denote a promotion to a senior position
exam: Identify the principal purpose of membership of a trade union. | To enable workers to negotiate collectively with employers on pay and conditions | To enable employers to recruit staff at reduced cost | To provide members with wages in place of those paid by their employer each month | To exempt members from the obligation to attend work
exam: It is given that employees have voted lawfully to strike. Ascertain which statement is correct. | The employees receive no pay for the duration of the strike | The employees continue to receive their full pay from the employer | The strike is unlawful notwithstanding the vote | The employees are thereby made redundant
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning an employment tribunal. | A body resembling a court which adjudicates disputes between employees and employers | It is a meeting convened by a trade union for the purpose of electing its officers | It is the process whereby a new employee is interviewed | It is a ceremony marking the retirement of an employee
